WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.583 --> 00:00:13.983
up, Ned!"

2
00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:18.186
I shouted as stones and arrows continued to rain down on us while we fled.

3
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:24.310
Ned couldn't stand to leave his provisions behind, so he tucked the hog and kangaroos under his arms

4
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.951
and ran as fast as he could.

5
00:00:26.992 --> 00:00:31.095
In an instant the three of us loaded the boat, pushed it out to sea, and started rowing.

6
00:00:31.115 --> 00:00:34.753
"That was close," said Ned, mopping his brow.

7
00:00:34.773 --> 00:00:36.835
"But at least they didn't get our food."

8
00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:43.539
We hadn't gone more than five hundred feet when a hundred natives appeared on the shore, yelling and

9
00:00:43.639 --> 00:00:45.180
gesturing in a hostile manner.

10
00:00:46.201 --> 00:00:48.022
They rushed into the water up to their waists.

11
00:00:49.043 --> 00:00:53.186
I watched to see if the noise would draw some men from the Nautilus to the platform.

12
00:00:53.906 --> 00:00:54.307
But no.

13
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:58.309
The enormous submarine lying offshore appeared completely deserted.

14
00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.772
Twenty minutes later we were onboard the Nautilus again.

15
00:01:02.568 --> 00:01:05.869
I rushed to the gallery, where Captain Nemo was playing the organ.

16
00:01:05.889 --> 00:01:09.010
"Captain!" I said, but he didn't hear me.

17
00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.310
I touched his hand.

18
00:01:12.311 --> 00:01:13.891
He started and turned around.

19
00:01:13.911 --> 00:01:15.652
"Ah, Professor!

20
00:01:16.072 --> 00:01:16.832
Did you have a good hunt?"

21
00:01:17.872 --> 00:01:21.553
"Yes, but I'm afraid we've been pursued by some hostile natives."

22
00:01:22.594 --> 00:01:27.455
"Really?" Captain Nemo turned back to his keyboard, more interested in that than in my story.

23
00:01:27.475 --> 00:01:29.375
"How many do you think were after you?"

24
00:01:29.395 --> 00:01:31.196
"At least a hundred."

25
00:01:32.064 --> 00:01:33.305
The captain began to play again.

26
00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:35.286
"I wouldn't worry, Professor.

27
00:01:35.786 --> 00:01:40.028
All the natives of New Guinea could assemble on this shore, and the Nautilus would have nothing to

28
00:01:40.069 --> 00:01:45.331
fear." The captain's confidence persuaded me, and I went to bed that night without any dread of the

29
00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:51.015
natives. The sub's hatches were open, so the natives could have easily slipped into the interior of the

30
00:01:51.035 --> 00:01:56.117
submarine. But they must have been frightened by the sight of the long, dark monster aground in their

31
00:01:56.157 --> 00:02:00.620
bay. At six o'clock the next morning, I returned to the platform.

32
00:02:01.632 --> 00:02:05.033
The natives had taken advantage of low tide to come out on the coral.

33
00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:09.754
There must have been five or six hundred of them within several hundred feet of the sub.

34
00:02:10.735 --> 00:02:16.076
Nearly all of them were armed with bows, arrows, and shields, and they carried nets over their shoulders

35
00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.096
containing stones.

36
00:02:18.077 --> 00:02:23.638
The women wore grass skirts while some of the chiefs wore necklaces of red and white glass beads.

37
00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:26.919
One chief examined the Nautilus attentively.

38
00:02:27.859 --> 00:02:32.293
He was probably a man of high rank because he was draped in a mat of banana leaves

39
00:02:32.653 --> 00:02:34.054
set off with brilliant colors.

40
00:02:34.995 --> 00:02:39.457
During low tide the natives roamed near the Nautilus, but they weren't troublesome.

41
00:02:40.418 --> 00:02:45.380
They frequently gestured to me and repeated a phrase as if they were inviting me to go onshore.

42
00:02:46.221 --> 00:02:47.401
I declined to do so.

43
00:02:48.342 --> 00:02:51.303
When the tide rose at eleven o'clock, they went back on land.

44
00:02:52.024 --> 00:02:55.146
I noticed that now there were even more of them on the island.

45
00:02:56.546 --> 00:02:59.408
Having nothing to do, I called for Conseil to bring me a net.

46
00:03:00.352 --> 00:03:04.353
We spent the next two hours dragging for shells but found nothing unusual.

47
00:03:05.313 --> 00:03:09.855
But then when I least expected it, I put my hand on a rare specimen.

48
00:03:09.875 --> 00:03:11.935
"Conseil, look at this!"

49
00:03:12.475 --> 00:03:14.016
I cried, holding up the shell.

50
00:03:14.036 --> 00:03:17.777
"Oh, you have found something special indeed!"

51
00:03:18.137 --> 00:03:20.798
replied Conseil as he took the shell from my hand.

52
00:03:21.838 --> 00:03:27.180
Conseil and I were absorbed in studying the valuable shell when a stone broke the precious object in

53
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:27.980
Conseil's hand.

54
00:03:29.032 --> 00:03:33.615
I uttered a cry of despair while Conseil picked up his gun and fired at a native who

55
00:03:33.655 --> 00:03:35.156
was still holding a slingshot.

56
00:03:36.057 --> 00:03:39.640
Conseil's shot broke a bracelet of amulets encircling the man's arm.

57
00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.021
"Conseil!" I cried.

58
00:03:42.041 --> 00:03:47.045
"No shell—not even a valuable one—is worth a man's life!"

59
00:03:47.065 --> 00:03:48.886
"The scoundrel!"

60
00:03:49.387 --> 00:03:50.127
snapped Conseil.

61
00:03:50.147 --> 00:03:53.429
"I wish he'd shattered my shoulder instead.

62
00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:54.790
Let's start the attack."

63
00:03:55.771 --> 00:03:56.852
I strongly disagreed.

64
00:03:57.216 --> 00:04:02.238
We were still discussing the matter when I looked up and saw that twenty canoes had surrounded the

65
00:04:02.298 --> 00:04:07.919
sub. Suddenly a shower of arrows hit the sub, and Conseil and I fled down the hatch.

66
00:04:09.039 --> 00:04:12.500
I went to the gallery but found no one there, so I knocked on the door of the

67
00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:13.261
captain's cabin.

68
00:04:13.281 --> 00:04:15.201
"Come in," he said.

69
00:04:16.142 --> 00:04:21.043
I entered and found Captain Nemo in the midst of some complicated mathematical calculations.

70
00:04:21.063 --> 00:04:24.424
"I'm disturbing you," I said politely.

71
00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.204
"That is true, Professor," Captain Nemo replied, "but you must have a serious reason for doing so."

72
00:04:31.224 --> 00:04:32.665
"Yes, sir.

73
00:04:33.025 --> 00:04:37.628
The natives have surrounded us with their canoes and in a few minutes they will certainly attack us!"

74
00:04:37.648 --> 00:04:41.071
"Well, then we must close all the hatches."

75
00:04:41.871 --> 00:04:44.833
Captain Nemo pressed a button to transmit an order to the crew.

76
00:04:44.853 --> 00:04:49.476
"You don't think their arrows could have any effect on the Nautilus, do you, Professor?"

77
00:04:49.857 --> 00:04:50.357
he asked me.

78
00:04:51.338 --> 00:04:54.640
"Remember, cannonballs from the Abraham Lincoln failed to pierce it."

79
00:04:55.664 --> 00:04:56.105
"No, sir.

80
00:04:56.425 --> 00:05:00.709
But I worry that the natives will get into the sub tomorrow morning when you open the hatches

81
00:05:00.749 --> 00:05:01.689
to take in fresh air."

82
00:05:01.709 --> 00:05:03.351
"Let them come in.

83
00:05:04.011 --> 00:05:07.634
I see no reason to stop them, and I don't want my visit here to cost the life

84
00:05:07.735 --> 00:05:10.016
of even one native," said Captain Nemo.

85
00:05:10.036 --> 00:05:15.321
"By the way, Professor, we'll be departing tomorrow afternoon at exactly 2:40."

86
00:05:16.402 --> 00:05:20.205
He bowed and I knew he was dismissing me, so I returned to my cabin.

87
00:05:21.206 --> 00:05:27.586
That night I had trouble sleeping because the natives were stomping on the platform and uttering deafening cries.

88
00:05:29.187 --> 00:05:32.949
When I woke up the next morning, I could tell that the air had been renewed.

89
00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:38.592
The natives were still up on the platform, so Captain Nemo must have released some air from the

90
00:05:38.652 --> 00:05:40.493
reservoirs instead of opening the hatches.

91
00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:46.937
I worked in my room all morning without seeing Captain Nemo or seeing any evidence that we would

92
00:05:46.957 --> 00:05:47.557
be departing.

93
00:05:48.578 --> 00:05:53.665
At 2:30 I went into the gallery; in only ten more minutes it would be high tide.

94
00:05:54.626 --> 00:06:00.151
If Captain Nemo were right, the Nautilus would soon be afloat once more; if not, we would be

95
00:06:00.271 --> 00:06:02.232
stuck here for who knows how long.

96
00:06:03.213 --> 00:06:06.836
I felt some vibrations as the ship's keel grated against the coral.

97
00:06:07.797 --> 00:06:10.940
At 2:35 Captain Nemo entered the gallery.

98
00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.322
"We're going to start," he said.

99
00:06:13.342 --> 00:06:16.104
"I've given the order to open the hatches."

100
00:06:16.124 --> 00:06:17.786
"And the natives?"

101
00:06:18.226 --> 00:06:18.506
I said.

102
00:06:18.526 --> 00:06:20.048
"What about them?"

103
00:06:21.524 --> 00:06:24.185
"Won't they climb inside the Nautilus once the hatches are open?"

104
00:06:25.406 --> 00:06:27.767
"No. Follow me and see what happens."

105
00:06:28.807 --> 00:06:31.028
Captain Nemo led the way to the central staircase.

106
00:06:32.069 --> 00:06:36.411
Ned and Conseil were there, watching as the crew members opened the hatch to the platform.

107
00:06:37.471 --> 00:06:40.233
Twenty fierce faces peered down at us.

108
00:06:40.973 --> 00:06:46.275
But the first native who placed his hand on the stair rail jumped back, screaming and holding his

109
00:06:46.335 --> 00:06:47.136
hand in pain.

110
00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.302
Ned rushed onto the staircase and the moment he seized the railing with both hands, he fell to

111
00:06:53.322 --> 00:06:53.782
the ground.

112
00:06:53.802 --> 00:06:56.243
"I've been struck by lightning!"

113
00:06:56.683 --> 00:06:57.164
he cried.

114
00:06:58.204 --> 00:07:00.025
Now I understood what was going on.

115
00:07:00.705 --> 00:07:05.747
The railings were electrified wires, so anyone who touched them got a terrible shock.

116
00:07:06.627 --> 00:07:12.430
The natives made a hasty retreat; we went to Ned's aid; and Nautilus, lifted by the tide at

117
00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.671
exactly 2:40, moved away from the coral bed.

118
00:07:16.571 --> 00:07:22.155
Our speed increased, and we finally passed safely out of the dangerous Torres Strait.
